    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d727">

                <pb facs="1433" n="1421" sameAs="frus:frus1945v02/pg1421"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">840.50/2–2145: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in the
                        United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-02-26T18:00:00-04:00">February 26,
                            1945—6 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1463. Be Embassy’s 1790, February 21.<anchor n="47" xml:id="d727-f1"/> For
                    Hawkins. Department has no information as to whether French would agree to
                    proceed with EEC and ECO conversations without Soviet attendance.</p>
                <p>Department did not intend to discuss with British in Washington the U.K.
                        memorandum<anchor n="48" xml:id="d727-f2"/> on ECO in view of Department’s
                    feeling that any detailed discussions in Washington would embarrass the conduct
                    of discussions in London by Hawkins.</p>
                <p>With regard to membership, Department’s suggestion that members be restricted at
                    the present time to Western European Allies was based partly on difficulties
                    which would result from attempt to include Polish representative or neutrals
                    (see Department’s 10129, December 2<anchor n="49" xml:id="d727-f3"/>), partly on
                    fact that transportation between Western Europe and other parts of the continent
                    is extremely restricted and <pb facs="1434" n="1422" xml:id="pg1422"/> partly on
                    Department’s view that there would be more chance of successfully setting up an
                    effective mechanism if a modest goal were sought. If and when conditions
                    warranted, representatives of areas other than Western Europe might be included
                    if the more limited objectives suggested by the Department had been
                    achieved.</p>
                <p>Embassy will note that last paragraph of Department’s 578 of January 26<anchor
                        n="50" xml:id="d727-f4"/> suggested as a possible topic of discussion the
                    establishing of a committee for Southeastern Europe. Also refer to Department’s
                    393 to Moscow, repeated to London as 1383.<anchor n="51" xml:id="d727-f5"/>
                </p>
                <p>The Department desires to re-emphasize its dislike of economic regionalism. If
                    the need for an organization like EEC continues into the period when it is
                    practicable to extend its scope to include all continental Europe, the
                    Department would prefer such extension rather than the maintenance of two or
                    more regional groups.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
